    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p79-q40.a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the particles meet after 4 seconds, and then again after a further 5 seconds of motion.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "They meet at $t=4$ and $t=9$ seconds: $x_A(4)=x_B(4)=2$ and $x_A(9)=x_B(9)=1$.",
          "worked": "At a meeting, $x_A(t)=x_B(t)$. Put $s=\\sqrt{t}\\geq0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}4-s&=\\frac{10}{s^{2}+1}\\\\(4-s)(s^{2}+1)&=10\\\\s^{3}-4s^{2}+s+6&=0\\\\(s-2)(s-3)(s+1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nSince $s\\geq0$, $s=2$ or $s=3$, giving $t=4$ or $t=9$. Also, $x_A(4)=x_B(4)=2$ and $x_A(9)=x_B(9)=1$. The second meeting is $9-4=5$ seconds after the first.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p79-q40.b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Which particle is moving faster when they meet for the first time?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$B$ is faster: $v_A(4)=-\\dfrac14\\text{ m s}^{-1}$ and $v_B(4)=-\\dfrac25\\text{ m s}^{-1}$, so $\\lvert v_B(4)\\rvert>\\lvert v_A(4)\\rvert$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}v_A(t)&=x_A'(t)=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\\\v_B(t)&=x_B'(t)=-\\frac{10}{(t+1)^{2}}\\\\v_A(4)&=-\\frac14\\text{ m s}^{-1}\\\\v_B(4)&=-\\frac25\\text{ m s}^{-1}\\end{align*}$$\nBecause $\\frac25>\\frac14$, particle $B$ is moving faster.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p79-q40.c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "How about when they meet the second time?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$A$ is faster: $v_A(9)=-\\dfrac16\\text{ m s}^{-1}$ and $v_B(9)=-\\dfrac1{10}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$, so $\\lvert v_A(9)\\rvert>\\lvert v_B(9)\\rvert$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}v_A(9)&=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt9}=-\\frac16\\text{ m s}^{-1}\\\\v_B(9)&=-\\frac{10}{(9+1)^{2}}=-\\frac1{10}\\text{ m s}^{-1}\\end{align*}$$\nBecause $\\frac16>\\frac1{10}$, particle $A$ is moving faster.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
